# spawn-shapes.manifest — the reviewed disposition of every `tmux new-window`
# call site in this repo. your-org/nexus-code#789.
#
# Columns (TAB-separated):
#   1 site        <repo-relative-path>#<ordinal> — the Nth `new-window` call
#                 site in that file. An ORDINAL, not a line number: keyed on
#                 lines, every row in a file moves when anything above it is
#                 edited, so unrelated work reddens the suite and the only
#                 practical response is to regenerate — and a manifest that is
#                 regenerated reflexively is a rubber stamp. Observed on this
#                 very branch: editing `_harness.sh` broke 5 of its own rows.
#                 `spawn-shapes.sh` emits the current line as a trailing
#                 `line=<n>` field for navigation, which the drift test
#                 deliberately does NOT compare.
#   2 form        bare | command | indirect      — FACT, from spawn-shapes.sh
#   3 root        shell | agent | n/a            — the REVIEWED pane root
#   4 sendkeys    sendkeys | no-sendkeys         — FACT, from spawn-shapes.sh
#   5 disposition production-spawn | harness-spawn | fixture-other |
#                 production-other | not-a-spawn
#   6 reason      why that disposition — required, and reviewed
#
# Columns 1, 2 and 4 are FACTS regenerated by `spawn-shapes.sh`;
# `test-spawn-shape-manifest.sh` fails when they drift, so a NEW call site lands
# here unclassified and RED. Column 3 is a VERDICT: the classifier can only
# derive the pane root when the command is a literal, and production's launchers
# all arrive through a variable — so where it derives `unknown` a human says what
# the variable holds, and where it derives `shell`/`agent` the manifest is
# checked AGAINST it and may not quietly disagree.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `absent` is the one kill-authorising pane state, and
# every `absent` decision is a reading of what sits under `pane_pid` and when.
# That is fixed by `new-window`, and it comes out differently for the two shapes
# production actually uses:
#
#   root=shell  `spawn-worker.sh` — bare new-window, then send-keys. The pane
#               root is a shell that outlives the agent, `#{pane_dead}` stays 0
#               forever, and the agent is a DESCENDANT that arrives seconds
#               later. Every worker on the board.
#   root=agent  `_respawn.sh` — the launcher IS the new-window command. tmux
#               flips `#{pane_dead}` the instant it exits.
#
# Until `#789` the shared integration harness modelled ONLY root=agent. The
# boot-window `absent` of `#777` was therefore not merely untested but
# UNTESTABLE there — the shape that produces it never occurred — which is how it
# survived `#55`, `#72`, `#643` and a dedicated regression suite. Nothing
# anywhere recorded that gap, so nothing could go red about it.
#
# THE ASSERTION THIS FILE MAKES CHECKABLE: every `root` value present among
# `production-spawn` rows must also be present among `harness-spawn` rows. Add a
# third production spawn shape with no harness counterpart and the suite fails,
# naming the unmodelled root. That is the coverage boundary as DATA — prose
# cannot be made to fail.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT CLAIM. That the harness models a shape FAITHFULLY, only
# that it models one at all; fidelity is asserted by
# `test-integration/test-spawn-busy-idle-absent.sh`, which pins `#{pane_dead}`
# per shape at the moment the agent dies. And nothing here reaches a pane root
# decided by a non-default `default-command`, or a `new-window` behind a wrapper
# whose name does not contain `tmux` — see spawn-shapes.sh's coverage boundary.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
monitor/cc-harness/_lib.sh#1	command	agent	sendkeys	harness-spawn	cc-harness spawns the REAL claude against a mock API; $launch is an `env … $CLAUDE_BIN …` string, so the agent is the pane root.
monitor/cc-harness/lint-no-tmux-server-kill.sh#1	command	n/a	no-sendkeys	not-a-spawn	A quoted lint FIXTURE string ("… && tmux new-window -dn %s"); the linter matches on it, nothing executes it.
monitor/spawn-worker.sh#1	bare	shell	sendkeys	production-spawn	The trust-overlay RECOVERY respawn (your-org/nexus-code#1334, _sw_trust_respawn): after the kill and a read-back re-seed, bare new-window under the SAME name, remain-on-exit, then send-keys the kept launcher — the worker spawn shape re-created. Ordinals shifted here when this site was added ABOVE the two production sites; the four rows below were re-attributed, not renumbered blind.
monitor/spawn-worker.sh#2	command	n/a	sendkeys	not-a-spawn	Diagnostic string in _sw_trust_respawn ("recovery: tmux new-window failed / returned no window id"). Tokenises as a command form; it is a printf.
monitor/spawn-worker.sh#3	bare	shell	sendkeys	production-spawn	The --resume spawn path. Bare new-window, then send-keys the generated launcher: pane root is the login shell.
monitor/spawn-worker.sh#4	command	n/a	sendkeys	not-a-spawn	Diagnostic string on the --resume path ("tmux new-window failed / returned no window id"). Tokenises as a command form; it is an echo.
monitor/spawn-worker.sh#5	bare	shell	sendkeys	production-spawn	THE worker spawn. Bare new-window, remain-on-exit, send-keys the launcher. Every worker on the board.
monitor/spawn-worker.sh#6	command	n/a	sendkeys	not-a-spawn	Diagnostic string on the fresh path, as the --resume one.
monitor/svc.sh#1	command	n/a	no-sendkeys	not-a-spawn	Inside a heredoc printed to stderr — a REFUSAL message telling the operator what to run. Not a call.
monitor/watcher/entry.sh#1	bare	n/a	no-sendkeys	not-a-spawn	Inside a heredoc printed to stderr — the "run it in another window" hint. Not a call.
monitor/watcher/_respawn.sh#2	command	n/a	sendkeys	not-a-spawn	Log-message string ("tmux new-window failed for '$target'").
monitor/watcher/_respawn.sh#1	command	agent	sendkeys	production-spawn	Orchestrator respawn. $launcher is the generated /tmp/nexus-respawn-launch-*.sh, which ends in `exec claude`, so the pane root becomes the agent.
monitor/watcher/test-bootstrap-recover.sh#1	bare	shell	sendkeys	fixture-other	Inside a stub spawn-worker.sh that only has to make a WINDOW APPEAR; nothing classifies its pane.
monitor/watcher/test-integration/_harness.sh#1	command	agent	sendkeys	harness-spawn	harness_spawn_worker_shape `command`: `exec env … $HARNESS_BIN/claude`. Models _respawn.sh.
monitor/watcher/test-integration/_harness.sh#2	command	shell	sendkeys	harness-spawn	harness_open_worker_window: `exec <sh>` leaves a lone shell at the pane root, then harness_send_worker_launcher send-keys the launcher. Models spawn-worker.sh (#789).
monitor/watcher/test-integration/test-graceful-exit-relaunch.sh#1	command	agent	no-sendkeys	harness-spawn	Re-spawns the stub claude into a recycled window by the command form.
monitor/watcher/test-integration/test-realmodel-apispoof.sh#1	command	agent	no-sendkeys	harness-spawn	cc-harness scenario; $launch is the real claude invocation.
monitor/watcher/test-integration/test-realmodel-overlimit.sh#1	command	agent	no-sendkeys	harness-spawn	cc-harness scenario; $launch is the real claude invocation.
monitor/watcher/test-integration/test-slow-grind-respawn.sh#1	command	n/a	no-sendkeys	not-a-spawn	Inside the PATH-shadow tmux wrapper: the forced-failure message it prints instead of spawning.
monitor/watcher/test-pane-state.sh#1	command	shell	no-sendkeys	fixture-other	Builds a window on a private socket to exercise the #140 bogus-index refusal; the pane is never expected to host an agent.
monitor/watcher/test-bash-footgun-guard.sh#1	bare	n/a	no-sendkeys	not-a-spawn	Guard PAYLOAD string (your-org/nexus-code#889): a JSON .tool_input.command handed to bash-footgun-guard to assert the tmux rows fire. Nothing executes tmux; this suite never starts a server.
monitor/watcher/test-bash-footgun-guard.sh#2	bare	n/a	no-sendkeys	not-a-spawn	Guard PAYLOAD string, as #1 — the CONTROL asserting a targeted kill chained with new-window stays clean.
monitor/watcher/test-respawn.sh#1	command	n/a	sendkeys	not-a-spawn	assert_contains needle — asserts the RECORDED tmux invocation, does not make one.
